{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-109731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,x⟩"] ["math" "⟨2\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,x⟩"] ["math" "⟨x,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩"] ["math" "⟨x,\\,2\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩"] ["math" "⟨x,\\,\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi (1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨x,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\text{_______},\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$ What is the second component of $r?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,4],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cour