1875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [-2,2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-109721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-109721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2x-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\pi (4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "