gned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z"] ["math" "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}"] ["math" "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{e}^{z}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi /4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "{cos}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "{sec}^{2}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{3}"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ and    \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince $f={e}^{1-{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2z{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}"] ["math" "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2z}{{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}}"] ["math" "\\frac{z}{({e}^{1-{z}^{2}})^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2z{e}^{2(1-{z}^{2})}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\text{_______}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [1,4]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [1,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{y}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{y}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={cos}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd  component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x={cos}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{2}⁡y"] ["math" "-{sin}^{2}⁡2y"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y=\\frac{x}{x+1}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x}{x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5