rname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1+{y}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2y.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-106058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-106058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-106058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-106058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{4}}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{{z}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}z"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161095" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=z\\sqrt{z}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x=z\\sqrt{z}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=z\\sqrt{z},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fb8d034be59ac339cfd7fb0378a92b53963025c0e6c52261f7e13601cdbbd48e"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{2}\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{z+{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{z}^{3}\\sqrt{1+z\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "q-161097" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\sqrt{z+\\frac{9}{4}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/state.json" "file_sha256" "9005c98b808251cef6900ad9c81f5c6755ee6660c1f7a7a54bd8bee427139fdf"} {"question" "e-8879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-8879.json" "file_sha256" "d0ba35d1b65dca97fc7003fa8f856052e5bca2613fbf39da94f1e2c50d8136ed"} {"question" "e-8879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in \\left[0,1\\right].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: x^2 \\cos\\theta, \\: x^2\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\nx \\in \\left[0, 1\\right],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-8879.json" "file_sha256" "d0ba35d1b65dca97fc7003fa8f856052e5bca2613fbf39da94f1e2c50d8136ed"} {"question" "e-8880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\cos{y}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y \\in [0, \\pi].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-8880.json" "file_sha256" "f355ad966436df51c83a49034b0a1162e7ef73c3a241ae53fa89a4024a416c57"} {"question" "e-8880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y) = 1-\\cos{y} \\geq 0$ for all $y \\in [0, \\pi].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta) = \n\\big\\langle (1-\\cos{y}) \\cos\\theta, \\: \ny, \\: \n(1-\\cos{y}) \\sin\\theta \n\\big\\rangle,\n\\quad\ny \\in[0, \\pi],\n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-8880.json" "file_sha256" "f355ad966436df51c83a49034b0a1162e7ef73c3a241ae53fa89a4024a416c57"} {"question" "e-12364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-12364.json" "file_sha256" "19fb352cca76b33d626c46e2c8db9fbdef8a37ce08ac684460d1dbfaea56a95c"} {"question" "e-12364" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $y=e^{x/2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=e^{x/2},$ and \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=e^{x/2},$ we obtain $f' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{e}^{x/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is $\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-12364.json" "file_sha256" "19fb352cca76b33d626c46e2c8db9fbdef8a37ce08ac684460d1dbfaea56a95c"} {"question" "e-12365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-12365.json" "file_sha256" "dc9f7c8c1a1c277ffb2d76f24a7adaf9e668fece931afe2444feabcd09c32304"} {"question" "e-12365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $x=\\sin z \\geq 0$ for all $z \\in [0, \\pi].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sin z,$ and\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (z,\\theta) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi , \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=\\sin z,$ we obtain $f'(z) = \\cos z.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/example-12365.json" "file_sha256" "dc9f7c8c1a1c277ffb2d76f24a7adaf9e668fece931afe2444feabcd09c32304"}] "usage" {"q-161069" [] "e-12365" [] "e-12364" [] "q-106058" [] "q-161095" [] "e-8879" [] "e-8880" []} "basis" 2590 "retractions" [[17592186172311 :question/problem "$⟨\\cos^{2} y\\cos θ, _______, \\cos^{2} y\\sin θ⟩$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x = \\cos^{2} y$ by $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y ∈ [- \\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?"] [17592186172164 :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x = 1 + y^{2}$ is rotated by $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y ∈ [- 1, 1]$."] [17592186172312 :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y = x^{4}$ is rotated by $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x ∈ [- 2, 2]$."] [17592186171794 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right]$. What is $\\mathbf{r}$?"] [17592186171794 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3372/Source/Images/34f3efad20bb1b36ba124e1cf3ca11d1.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$[MATH: r = ⟨x, f(x)\\cos θ, f(x)\\sin θ⟩, x ∈ [a, b], θ ∈ [0, 2π).]$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\ny=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left[0,1\\right]\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: x^2 \\cos\\theta, \\: x^2\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right], \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$"] [17592186171795 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\cos{y}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y \\in [0, \\pi]$. What is $\\mathbf{r}$?"] [17592186171795 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3372/Source/Images/e415b25ee1a73d8953b38a5cec1ac802.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$[MATH: r = ⟨f(y)\\cos θ, y, f(y)\\sin θ⟩, y ∈ [a, b], θ = [0, 2π).]$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nx=f(y) = 1-\\cos{y} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $y \\in [0, \\pi]$.\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta) = \\big\\langle (1-\\cos{y}) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: (1-\\cos{y}) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y \\in[0, \\pi], \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$"] [17592186171644 :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n$\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle$.\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?"] [17592186171644 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\ny=e^{x/2} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in [0,2]$. The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr = ⟨x, f(x)\\cos θ, f(x)\\sin θ⟩, (x, θ) ∈ D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x}, \\frac{∂y}{∂x}, \\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩ = ⟨1, f^{′}\\cos θ, f^{′}\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\nr_{θ}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂y}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂z}{∂θ}⟩ = ⟨0,-f\\sin θ, f\\cos θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ 0 & -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ 0 & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & f^{′}\\cos θ \\\\ 0 & -f\\sin θ\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ff^{′}\\cos^{2} θ + ff^{′}\\sin^{2} θ,-f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ff^{′},-f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf=e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nf' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{x},-e^{x/2}\\cos θ,-e^{x/2}\\sin θ⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x\n$$"] [17592186171645 :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi]$."] [17592186171645 :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nx=\\sin z \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $z \\in [0, \\pi]$. The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr = ⟨f(z)\\cos θ, f(z)\\sin θ, z⟩, (z, θ) ∈ D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(z)=\\sin z\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(z,\\theta) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{z}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z}, \\frac{∂y}{∂z}, \\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩ = ⟨f^{′}\\cos θ, f^{′}\\sin θ, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{θ}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂y}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂z}{∂θ}⟩ = ⟨ - f\\sin θ, f\\cos θ, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ & 1 \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\sin θ & 1 \\\\ f\\cos θ & 0\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & 1 \\\\ -f\\sin θ & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ, f^{′}f\\cos^{2} θ + f^{′}f\\sin^{2} θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ, f^{′}f⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′}‖ &= \\sqrt{(-f\\cos θ)^{2} + (-f\\sin θ)^{2} + (f^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{f^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) + f^{2}(f^{′})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{f^{2} + f^{2}(f^{′})^{2}} \\\\\n&= f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∬_(S)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)‖r_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′}‖dzdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dzdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}∫_{0}^{2π}dθdz \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}} \\cdot θ \\mid _{0}^{2π}dz \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(z)=\\sin z\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nf'(z) = \\cos z\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2π∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dz &= 2π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin z\\sqrt{1 + (\\cos z)^{2}}dz \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin z\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} z}dz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168188 17592186168189 17592186168190 17592186168191 17592186168192 17592186169070 17592186169071 17592186169072 17592186169073 17592186169074 17592186169076 17592186169077 17592186169078 17592186169079 17592186169080] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168